From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 20:08:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm: do not tear down shared-VM mappings on GEM handle
 close

msm_gpu_create_private_vm() hands a context the global VM whenever the
target cannot give it a private one -- a5xx and older have no per-process
pagetables at all, and even on a6xx it is the fallback when creating one
fails. Since the drm_gpuvm conversion in 111fdd2198e6 ("drm/msm:
drm_gpuvm conversion"), msm_gem_close() unmaps and closes the object's
VMA in ctx->vm on every handle close. Where ctx->vm is the global VM,
that VMA is not the closing context's to free.

msm_gem_prime_import() returns the *same* drm_gem_object for a dmabuf
this device exported, so a buffer shared between a compositor and its
client is one GEM object with one drm_gpuva in that one VM. Whichever
process calls GEM_CLOSE first unmaps the buffer -- and frees its iova --
out from under the other, whose userspace still has that iova baked into
cmdstream. The GPU then reads an unmapped address; on a540 the CP
consumes the terminated transaction and the result is

  *** gpu fault: iova=00000000122d9280 flags=0 (0,0,0,0)
  msm_dpu c901000.display-controller: CP | opcode error | possible opcode=0x00000000
  [drm:recover_worker] *ERROR* 5.4.0.1: offending task: phoc

Before 111fdd2198e6 msm_gem_close() did no teardown at all, which is why
this only appears from v6.17 onwards.

Skip the teardown for contexts on the global VM and let the existing
@vma_ref drop handle it instead: it already counts open handles and live
exports for exactly this purpose, so reaching zero is the first moment at
which nobody can hold the iova any more. That restores the pre-v6.17
lifetime for these mappings without leaking them -- the vm_bo holds a
reference to the object, so simply never closing the VMA would keep the
object alive forever. Targets with private pagetables are unaffected:
userspace buffers never live in gpu->vm there, so the added
put_iova_spaces() finds nothing.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c | 28 +++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c
index 9f7fbe577abb..1b78cf032357 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c
@@ -49,6 +49,7 @@ static void put_iova_spaces(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct drm_gpuvm *vm,
 
 static void msm_gem_close(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct drm_file *file)
 {
+	struct msm_drm_private *priv = obj->dev->dev_private;
 	struct msm_context *ctx = file->driver_priv;
 	struct drm_exec exec;
 
@@ -59,8 +60,15 @@ static void msm_gem_close(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct drm_file *file)
 	 * If VM isn't created yet, nothing to cleanup.  And in fact calling
 	 * put_iova_spaces() with vm=NULL would be bad, in that it will tear-
 	 * down the mappings of shared buffers in other contexts.
+	 *
+	 * The same is true when the context shares the global VM, which is
+	 * what msm_gpu_create_private_vm() hands out on targets without
+	 * per-process pagetables: the VMA torn down here belongs just as much
+	 * to every other context, and a self-imported dmabuf is the *same*
+	 * GEM object in all of them.  Leave those to msm_gem_vma_put(), once
+	 * the last handle and the last export are gone.
 	 */
-	if (!ctx->vm)
+	if (!ctx->vm || (priv->gpu && ctx->vm == priv->gpu->vm))
 		return;
 
 	/*
@@ -95,16 +103,30 @@ void msm_gem_vma_get(struct drm_gem_object *obj)
 void msm_gem_vma_put(struct drm_gem_object *obj)
 {
 	struct msm_drm_private *priv = obj->dev->dev_private;
+	struct drm_exec exec;
 
 	if (atomic_dec_return(&to_msm_bo(obj)->vma_ref))
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * Zero handles and no export left, so this is the first point at which
+	 * a mapping in the GPU's global VM can be freed without pulling it out
+	 * from under another context.  See msm_gem_close().  A no-op where
+	 * contexts get a private VM, as userspace buffers never land in
+	 * gpu->vm there.
+	 */
+	if (priv->gpu && priv->gpu->vm) {
+		dma_resv_wait_timeout(obj->resv, DMA_RESV_USAGE_BOOKKEEP, false,
+				      MAX_SCHEDULE_TIMEOUT);
+		msm_gem_lock_vm_and_obj(&exec, obj, priv->gpu->vm);
+		put_iova_spaces(obj, priv->gpu->vm, true, "vma_put");
+		drm_exec_fini(&exec);     /* drop locks */
+	}
+
 	if (!priv->kms)
 		return;
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_DRM_MSM_KMS
-	struct drm_exec exec;
-
 	msm_gem_lock_vm_and_obj(&exec, obj, priv->kms->vm);
 	put_iova_spaces(obj, priv->kms->vm, true, "vma_put");
 	drm_exec_fini(&exec);     /* drop locks */
